1)Explain the difference between Stateless and Stateful widgets with examples.
 Ans -> 
        Stateless Widget :-

        A Stateless widget is a widget that cannot change its data after the app starts.
        It is fixed and does not update automatically.

        Example:
           Text(
        "Hello Flutter",
          )

        In this example, the text stays the same.

        Features:
        Static data
        Cannot change UI dynamically
        Simple and lightweight
  
        Stateful Widget :-
  
        Stateful Widget

        A Stateful widget is a widget that can change its data during runtime.
        It updates the UI when the state changes.
 
        Example:
        int count = 0;

        When the value of count changes, the screen updates.

        Features:
        Dynamic data
        UI can change
        Uses setState().
 2)Describe the widget lifecycle and how state is managed in Stateful widgets. 
  Ans ->
        The widget lifecycle means the different stages a Stateful widget goes through from creation to removal.
        1. createState()

        This method creates the state object.

        createState()
        2. initState()
 
        Called only once when the widget starts.
  
        Used for:

        API calls
        Initial data setup
        @override
        void initState() {
         super.initState();
         }
        3. build()

        Builds the UI of the widget.

         @override
         Widget build(BuildContext context) {
        return Container();
        }
       4. setState()

       Updates the UI when data changes.

       setState(() {
        count++;
       });
      5. dispose()

         Called when the widget is removed from the screen.

        Used to:
   
       Close controllers
       Free memory
       @override
       void dispose() {
       super.dispose();
         }

3)List and describe five common Flutter layout widgets (e.g., Container, Column, Row).
  Ans ->
        1. Container

        Container is used to design and style widgets.
      
        It can add:

        Height
        Width
        Color
        Padding
        Margin
         Example:
        Container(
        height: 100,
        width: 100,
         color: Colors.blue,
         )
        2. Row

        Row arranges widgets horizontally.
 
        Example:
         Row(
           children: [
             Icon(Icons.home),
             Text("Home"),
            ],
          )
        3. Column

        Column arranges widgets vertically.

            Example:
         Column(
         children: [
             Text("Name"),
             Text("Email"),
            ],
          )
        4. Stack
   
         Stack places widgets on top of each other.

         Example:
          Stack(
         children: [
          Container(color: Colors.blue),
          Text("Hello"),
          ],
         )
       5. ListView
   
        ListView displays scrollable items.

       Example:
        ListView(
        children: [
         Text("Item 1"),
         Text("Item 2"),
         ],
         )

         These layout widgets help developers create different UI designs in Flutter.
